{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghdjeS8PjCeEVucredWJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 108 checks pass in 62 seconds, including the browser check and the four hub scenarios. I've addressed every gap the hook listed, but a few items are built as approximations rather than the plan's full versions, so I can't honestly say every feature is implemented to the plan's fidelity.\n\n## What was added this round\n\n- **Hair and hats:** braids, a bob and curls join the buns and puffs. Sun hats and visors are drawn. Ingrid has braids, Tess a bob, and Lola curls.\n- **Rash-guard sleeves:** drawn on the upper arms.\n- **Motion capture and IK:** the block plays an arms-up clip while the hands go over the net. Winning partners do a high five, with their hands meeting at shoulder height.\n- **Group photos:** a postcard holds the photographer and up to two others. The party's group photo is taken automatically.\n- **Voice Lab:** lists every line of each speaker, and speaks the one you pick.\n- **Barks:** each touch and each point won is voiced.\n- **House Edge:** a partner with at least good-friend affinity covers the shot, and calls \"I've got line!\".\n- **Pad combo:** L3 and R3 together reopen the dev panel once it has been opened, as F1 does.\n\n## Verification\n\n`node test/run.mjs` passes 108 checks in 62 seconds. This includes the browser check of the file and the four AI Hub scenarios. A new `features` suite covers the voice lab, barks, group photos, the hair extras and the block clip.\n\n## Still approximations\n\n- **Montage:** the seven beats are short drawn scenes with captions, not footage of the real game.\n- **Looks:** the swimsuit cuts, patterns, hair and hats are drawn shapes over the engine's rig, not new engine art.\n- **Motion:** motion capture plays on celebrations, shrugs, dives, blocks, idles and the title. It isn't on every move.\n- **Online:** the host runs the rules and the AI, as the plan specifies. Clients send claims, but the host has no client-side prediction.\n\n## Sections\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-04 01:24:24\n- Source: Dead or Alive Xtreme Beach Volleyball (Team Ninja / Tecmo, 2003)\n- Plan: PLAN.md\n- Genre: sports (2v2 beach volleyball), with a light life sim and minigames\n- Perspective: broadcast side-on court view with a Serve Cam toggle; three-quarter view for walkable places; isometric island map\n- Tech: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (two engine patches), plain JavaScript, the engine's motion runtime, Playwright 1.63.0, Node 24\n- Art direction: PS1-style pixel art, sunset palette, swimsuits drawn over the rig\n- Animation: engine rig poses and attack moves, 20 motion-capture clips, and arm IK\n- Audio: all synthesized in code, including Animalese voices and barks\n- Online: AI Hub shared-world session, with client claims checked by the host\n- Testing: the 108-check command described above\n- Research: engine docs, the AI Hub protocol and client; no web research\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); headless Chromium\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- Issues caught: about 16 game issues in the earlier rounds, plus two more this round: the AI Hub copy referenced an undefined variable (the harness caught it), and the dev panel's freeze stalled the fade.\n- Critical / game-breaking: 2 earlier: the Pool Hop slip that never recovered, and the black-screen freeze. Both are fixed and covered.\n- Most helpful: the headless full-match run and the AI Hub harness.\n- Least helpful: screenshot checks of small details, and fixed-wait key scripts, which can't hit timing windows.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n- Liked most: the fixed-step clock and headless mode, and the motion runtime, which attached to the rigs without engine changes.\n- Stuck or guessed wrong: the scene fade runs on game time, so a freeze set on entry stalls it. The pixel font lacks many symbols.\n- Most important to build myself: the referee, planner, timing judge, AI, text box with voices, the vacation rules, and the swimsuit overlays.\n- Hardest: making a ground-view match read as a side-on broadcast, and drawing swimsuits over a rig with no swimsuit outfit.\n- Engine changes: two marked `AOA:` (PlayStation symbols by default; a per-letter dialog voice hook the game does not use). The overlays, IK and motion runtime use the engine's public functions.\n- Change one thing: a way to make scene changes immediate in headless tests.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n- Liked most: the concrete numbers and the feature checklist, which made each gap easy to check.\n- Confusing: the plan and TASK.md disagreed on names and date; TASK.md was followed. The plan's scope is far larger than one session.\n- Hardest: keeping every system consistent at once, and the engine-level swimsuit and motion work.\n- Would change: ask for a priority order and a smaller first milestone, and state which parts may be simplified.\n\nThe exit interview is `exit-interview.json` in the work folder. It parses and fills every field.\n\nI can keep going on the approximations if you want them closer to the plan. The montage beats would need real footage of each scene, and the looks would need new engine art."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1684,"cache_read_input_tokens":692218,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1684},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"68c96722-ff85-4e7d-8e61-8c2d4dde53f4","uuid":"4c7b3400-50ee-484a-835a-c3ef09c2e720","timestamp":"2026-11-04T09:15:31.591Z","request_id":"req_011Cghdje2p4UvdiuDLP59qN"}
