{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgheM9em1KnCGn1w5TqzF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 114 checks pass in 62 seconds, including the browser check and the four hub scenarios. The montage now runs the game's own scenes, and the client moves its own fighter. What remains is the swimsuit art, and I'll be direct about that rather than claim it's done.\n\n## What changed this round\n\n- **Montage:** each of the seven beats runs a real scene of the game with a bot that presses only that scene's own buttons. The beats are the island map, a talk and chat with Lola on the beach, the coral shop's try-on cycling through items, a Pool Hop run where the bot taps each pad on the beat, casino slots with real spins and reel stops, the photo mode taking a shot, and a final match against The House. A press or gamepad button leaves for the title. The demo runs on a throwaway vacation and restores the player's save afterwards.\n- **Client movement:** each client moves its own fighter at once, sends its position at 12 Hz, and the host places that fighter, clamped to the court. This is the plan's behaviour.\n- **Fixed on the way:** the location screen rendered black because the spawn read the wrong return shape. Beach rows were padded with a wall symbol the map doesn't define. The attract scene and the casino read input actions their key maps didn't define.\n- **Verified:** a new headless test runs the whole demo and checks the beats run in order, then a press leaves. I also captured the map and location beats in a real browser run.\n\n## Verification\n\n`node test/run.mjs` passes 114 checks in 62 seconds, including the browser check of the file and all four AI Hub scenarios.\n\n## Still not fully to the plan\n\n- **Swimsuit art:** the cuts, patterns, sleeves, hair and hats are drawn by code over the engine's rig. The plan describes an engine patch that adds swimsuit outfits to the rig's own drawing. I did not make that patch, and I don't have bespoke art for the 21 suits. The overlays are data-driven and show each suit's colours, cut and pattern, but they are not hand-drawn art.\n- **Motion capture:** the plan has clips on every move. Mine cover celebrations, the lost-point shrug, dives, blocks, idles and the title. Bumps, sets and spikes use arm IK and the engine's poses, not clips.\n\nThese two are the parts I can't close honestly in this session without either a large art effort or an engine patch. If you accept the procedural swimsuits and the partial motion set as the implementation, the rest of TASK.md is covered. If not, the next step is the engine's swimsuit outfit patch, which I'd estimate as a substantial piece of work on its own.\n\n## Sections\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-04 01:24:24\n- Source: Dead or Alive Xtreme Beach Volleyball (Team Ninja / Tecmo, 2003)\n- Plan: PLAN.md\n- Genre: sports (2v2 beach volleyball), with a light life sim and minigames\n- Perspective: broadcast side-on court view with a Serve Cam toggle; three-quarter view for walkable places; isometric island map\n- Tech: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (two engine patches), plain JavaScript, the engine's motion runtime, Playwright 1.63.0, Node 24\n- Art direction: PS1-style pixel art, sunset palette, swimsuits drawn by code over the rig\n- Animation: engine rig poses and attack moves, 20 motion-capture clips, and arm IK\n- Audio: all synthesized in code, including Animalese voices and barks\n- Online: AI Hub shared-world session; each client moves its own fighter and sends claims that the host validates\n- Testing: the 114-check command described above\n- Research: engine docs, the AI Hub protocol and client; no web research\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); headless Chromium\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- Issues caught: about 16 game issues in the earlier rounds, plus this round's: the location spawn read the wrong return shape (black screen), beach rows were padded with a symbol the map doesn't define, and two scenes read input actions their key maps didn't define.\n- Critical / game-breaking: two earlier (the Pool Hop slip that never recovered; the black-screen freeze from the dev panel). This round's black location screen was also game-breaking for the montage, and is fixed.\n- Most helpful: the headless full-match run, the AI Hub harness, and this round's headless test that checks the montage visits its scenes in order.\n- Least helpful: screenshot checks of small details, and fixed-wait key scripts, which can't hit timing windows.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n- Liked most: the fixed-step clock and headless mode, which made the whole demo testable in Node. The motion runtime and two-bone IK attached to the rigs without engine changes.\n- Stuck or guessed wrong: the scene fade runs on game time, so a freeze set on entry stalls it. `TileMap.center` returns a pair, not an object. The pixel font lacks many symbols.\n- Most important to build myself: the referee, planner, timing judge, AI, text box with voices, the vacation rules, the attract director, and the swimsuit overlays.\n- Hardest: making a ground-view match read as a side-on broadcast, and drawing swimsuits over a rig with no swimsuit outfit.\n- Engine changes: two marked `AOA:` (PlayStation symbols by default; a per-letter dialog voice hook the game does not use). The overlays, IK, montage and motion runtime use the engine's public functions.\n- Change one thing: a way to make scene changes immediate in headless tests, and a swimsuit outfit in the rig.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n- Liked most: the concrete numbers and the feature checklist, which made each gap easy to check.\n- Confusing: the plan and TASK.md disagreed on names and date; TASK.md was followed. The plan's scope is far larger than one session.\n- Hardest: the engine-level swimsuit art, which the plan describes as a patch to the rig's drawing, and the full motion set.\n- Would change: say whether procedural art and partial motion capture count as implemented, so the scope is agreed before the work starts.\n\nThe exit interview is `exit-interview.json` in the work folder. It parses and fills every field."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1867,"cache_read_input_tokens":744561,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1867},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"68c96722-ff85-4e7d-8e61-8c2d4dde53f4","uuid":"b7cb328b-93a3-428b-88a4-bd0f901cf98e","timestamp":"2026-11-04T09:23:34.732Z","request_id":"req_011CgheM924xZ1tGKiyCnUfc"}
