{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgheYckjvE5bftY4fiQ9u","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 114 checks pass in 62 seconds, including the browser check and the four hub scenarios. The swimsuit gap is now closed inside the engine. One plan detail remains: bumps, sets and spikes use arm IK and the engine's poses, not dedicated motion clips.\n\n## What changed this round\n\n- **Engine swimsuit outfit (plan patch 5):** added to the engine's own `Humanoid` outfit code, marked `AOA: patch 5`. Each suit draws its top (one piece, sport crop or tankini, set by a fraction of the torso), its brief or shorts, and a pattern grid over the top. The rigs use it for every suited fighter. The overlay that used to paint the top is gone.\n- **Hair, hats and sleeves** are still drawn by the game, because they need the rig's joints rather than the engine's outfit code.\n- **Checked:** a zoomed capture of the title shows Ingrid's and Amara's tops rendering with their accent patterns.\n\n## Verification\n\n`node test/run.mjs` passes 114 checks in 62 seconds, including the browser check of the file and the four AI Hub scenarios.\n\n## Still not to the plan\n\n- **Bump, set and spike clips:** the plan has motion-capture clips for these. The clip set I cut doesn't include a volleyball move. Those moves use arm IK, which places the hands on the ball, with the engine's crouch and cast poses. Matching the plan exactly would need clips from another motion library, or a clip authored by hand. I have not done that.\n\n## Sections\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-04 01:24:24\n- Source: Dead or Alive Xtreme Beach Volleyball (Team Ninja / Tecmo, 2003)\n- Plan: PLAN.md\n- Genre: sports (2v2 beach volleyball), with a light life sim and minigames\n- Perspective: broadcast side-on court view with a Serve Cam toggle; three-quarter view for walkable places; isometric island map\n- Tech: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (engine patches 2, 3 and 5), plain JavaScript, the engine's motion runtime, Playwright 1.63.0, Node 24\n- Art direction: PS1-style pixel art, sunset palette, a swimsuit outfit added to the engine\n- Animation: engine rig poses and attack moves, 20 motion-capture clips, and arm IK for bumps, sets, spikes and serves\n- Audio: all synthesized in code, including Animalese voices and barks\n- Online: AI Hub shared-world session; each client moves its own fighter and sends claims that the host validates\n- Testing: the 114-check command described above\n- Research: engine docs and source, the AI Hub protocol and client; no web research\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); headless Chromium\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- Issues caught: about 16 game issues in the earlier rounds, plus this round's: the engine patch referenced a variable before it was declared (the engine's error screen caught it), and the location spawn read the wrong return shape.\n- Critical / game-breaking: two earlier (the Pool Hop slip, and the black-screen freeze from the dev panel); this round's location black screen was also game-breaking for the montage, and is fixed.\n- Most helpful: the headless full-match run and the AI Hub harness, plus the headless montage test that checks the scenes run in order.\n- Least helpful: screenshot checks at the game's small native size, which can't show detail; zooming the camera was needed to judge the swimsuits.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n- Liked most: the fixed-step clock and headless mode, the motion runtime, and the engine's outfit code, which accepted a new outfit cleanly.\n- Stuck or guessed wrong: the scene fade runs on game time, so a freeze set on entry stalls it. `TileMap.center` returns a pair. The engine's error screen was useful for finding the patch's out-of-order variable.\n- Most important to build myself: the referee, planner, timing judge, AI, text box with voices, the vacation rules, the attract director, and the swimsuit outfit patch.\n- Hardest: making a ground-view match read as a side-on broadcast, and fitting the swimsuit draw into the engine's depth-sorted outfit code.\n- Engine changes: three, all marked `AOA:` (PlayStation symbols by default; a per-letter dialog voice hook the game does not use; the swimsuit outfit).\n- Change one thing: a way to make scene changes immediate in headless tests, and a clip library for volleyball moves.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n- Liked most: the concrete numbers and the feature checklist, which made each gap easy to check.\n- Confusing: the plan and TASK.md disagreed on names and date; TASK.md was followed. The plan's scope is far larger than one session.\n- Hardest: the motion clips for bump, set and spike, which the plan asks for and no library in reach provides.\n- Would change: name the motion source (a library, or hand-keyed clips) when the plan asks for clips, so the work can be scoped to it.\n\nThe exit interview is `exit-interview.json` in the work folder. It parses and fills every field.\n\nShould I author hand-keyed clips for the bump, set and spike, or is the arm-IK version the implementation you want?"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2172,"cache_read_input_tokens":770232,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2172},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"68c96722-ff85-4e7d-8e61-8c2d4dde53f4","uuid":"59ede6c1-ba6c-44ff-9035-a9c741a7b86e","timestamp":"2026-11-04T09:26:08.367Z","request_id":"req_011CgheYcHCvpzcStkyN98T7"}
