{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RZw4jUezLpndm656xeAA2P","type":"tool_result","content":"/* =============================================================================\n * READABLE KEY POSES: the animation format my-3D2dge stores, and the one AI models (and people) read and edit.\n * A clip is a short list of key poses. Each pose names what an animator would: where the hips are, how the body,\n * chest and head turn and lean, how the shoulders reach and shrug, which way each arm and leg points with how much\n * elbow or knee bend, and how the feet point. It does not depend on body size, so the same clip fits any build, and\n * it mirrors by swapping L and R. Limbs are a direction plus a bend, not joint angles: an arm swinging through\n * straight down has no angle that flips, a bend in degrees rounds well, and an IK rig consumes exactly this.\n *\n * Shared by tools/anim-import.mjs (Node: it measures a captured body, fits each clip, writes the set) and the\n * browser (src/mocap/mocap.js: it rebuilds every body point from a set to draw or drive a rig). No dependencies.\n *   const rest = MocapReadable.measure(captureClips)          // a body's proportions, from its own clips\n *   const { clip } = MocapReadable.fit(rest, capClip, 30)     // key poses within 30 mm of the capture\n *   MocapReadable.text(clip)  MocapReadable.parse(text)  MocapReadable.mirror(clip)\n *   MocapReadable.pose(rest, clip, t, out)                    // 36 body points (mm) at time t\n * ============================================================================= */\nvar MocapReadable = (() => {\n'use \n----\n2: * READABLE KEY POSES: the animation format my-3D2dge stores, and the one AI models (and people) read and edit.\n3: * A clip is a short list of key poses. Each pose names what an animator would: where the hips are, how the body,\n4: * chest and head turn and lean, how the shoulders reach and shrug, which way each arm and leg points with how much\n5: * elbow or knee bend, and how the feet point. It does not depend on body size, so the same clip fits any build, and\n6: * it mirrors by swapping L and R. Limbs are a direction plus a bend, not joint angles: an arm swinging through\n7: * straight down has no angle that flips, a bend in degrees rounds well, and an IK rig consumes exactly this.\n8: *\n9: * Shared by tools/anim-import.mjs (Node: it measures a captured body, fits each clip, writes the set) and the\n10: * browser (src/mocap/mocap.js: it rebuilds every body point from a set to draw or drive a rig). No dependencies.\n11: *   const rest = MocapReadable.measure(captureClips)          // a body's proportions, from its own clips\n12: *   const { clip } = MocapReadable.fit(rest, capClip, 30)     // key poses within 30 mm of the capture\n13: *   MocapReadable.text(clip)  MocapReadable.parse(text)  MocapReadable.mirror(clip)\n14: *   MocapReadable.pose(rest, clip, t, out)                    // 36 body points (mm) at time t\n15: * ============================================================================= */\n26:// L and R are the figure's own sides. Directions are percentages and need not add up: [100, 0, 0] and [50, 0, 0] agree.\n27:// hips   [forward, right, up]   the pelvis, in percent of standing hip height (100 = standing, 55 = sitting on a chair)\n28:// body   [turn, lean, tilt]     the pelvis, degrees: turn + = toward the right, lean + = forward, tilt + = toward the right\n29:// chest  [turn, lean, tilt]     the chest, relative to the pelvis        head [turn, lean, tilt]  relative to the chest\n30:// shL shR    [reach, shrug]     degrees the shoulder swings forward (into a punch or an aim) and up, from rest\n31:// armL armR  [forward, out, up, bend, twist]  which way the arm points from the shoulder to the fist, in chest space\n32://            (out + = away from the body on that side; [0, 0, -100] hangs down, [100, 0, 0] punches ahead);\n33://            bend = elbow bend in degrees (0 = straight); twist = degrees the elbow swings round the arm from its\n34://            natural direction (back and down)\n35:// legL legR  [forward, out, up, bend, twist]  hip to ankle in pelvis space; bend: the knee; twist: from pointing forward\n36:// footL footR [down, out, toes]   degrees the foot points down from standing, turns outward, and the toes bend up\n37:// blade  [forward, right, up]   (sword clips) the direction of a blade held in the right fist, in chest space\n38:// root   [forward, right]       (clips that travel) how far the whole body has moved, in percent of standing hip height`;\n47:// a frame is [F, R, U]: its forward, right and up axes written in the parent's coordinates (f x r = u)\n55:// rotations as quaternions [x, y, z, w], to spread a bend over the spine and the neck\n106:// A twist is measured round the limb from the way its middle joint naturally bends. That direction is known for one\n107:// limb direction (REST: an arm hanging forward, out and down, its elbow bending back; a leg down, its knee forward)\n108:// and carried to the limb's actual direction by the shortest turn, so it moves smoothly with the limb everywhere\n109:// except straight opposite REST, where no arm or leg reaches (an arm behind the head across the back; a leg up behind).\n110:// (An earlier reference blended two fixed directions; where they faced apart it flipped, and in-betweens put the elbow\n111:// on the wrong side.) Both sides compute it from the stored direction, so they always agree.\n133:// the finger helpers only aim a blade: errors leave them out\n151: * measure(clips, tpose): proportions and rest offsets from the T-pose (or the first frame of the first clip), and how\n152: * much of the hips-to-chest turn each spine bone takes (and the neck between chest and head). That share differs per\n153: * library (how its animators posed the spine), so it is fitted from the clips themselves. The result is plain data.\n154: */\n288: * fit(R, cap, tol): a readable clip within tol mm of the capture. It starts from the position key frames and adds the\n289: * frame the readable in-betweening misses most, until the rest is within tol of what the format can capture at all\n290: * (a frame encoded as its own key): keys past the format's own limits are not worth their tokens.\n291: * Returns { clip, max, mean }: the worst and the average body-point error over the clip (mm).\n292: */","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"68c96722-ff85-4e7d-8e61-8c2d4dde53f4","uuid":"a94993e6-1ebe-4846-8b62-b4158620eabe","timestamp":"2026-11-04T09:26:15.145Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"/* =============================================================================\n * READABLE KEY POSES: the animation format my-3D2dge stores, and the one AI models (and people) read and edit.\n * A clip is a short list of key poses. Each pose names what an animator would: where the hips are, how the body,\n * chest and head turn and lean, how the shoulders reach and shrug, which way each arm and leg points with how much\n * elbow or knee bend, and how the feet point. It does not depend on body size, so the same clip fits any build, and\n * it mirrors by swapping L and R. Limbs are a direction plus a bend, not joint angles: an arm swinging through\n * straight down has no angle that flips, a bend in degrees rounds well, and an IK rig consumes exactly this.\n *\n * Shared by tools/anim-import.mjs (Node: it measures a captured body, fits each clip, writes the set) and the\n * browser (src/mocap/mocap.js: it rebuilds every body point from a set to draw or drive a rig). No dependencies.\n *   const rest = MocapReadable.measure(captureClips)          // a body's proportions, from its own clips\n *   const { clip } = MocapReadable.fit(rest, capClip, 30)     // key poses within 30 mm of the capture\n *   MocapReadable.text(clip)  MocapReadable.parse(text)  MocapReadable.mirror(clip)\n *   MocapReadable.pose(rest, clip, t, out)                    // 36 body points (mm) at time t\n * ============================================================================= */\nvar MocapReadable = (() => {\n'use \n----\n2: * READABLE KEY POSES: the animation format my-3D2dge stores, and the one AI models (and people) read and edit.\n3: * A clip is a short list of key poses. Each pose names what an animator would: where the hips are, how the body,\n4: * chest and head turn and lean, how the shoulders reach and shrug, which way each arm and leg points with how much\n5: * elbow or knee bend, and how the feet point. It does not depend on body size, so the same clip fits any build, and\n6: * it mirrors by swapping L and R. Limbs are a direction plus a bend, not joint angles: an arm swinging through\n7: * straight down has no angle that flips, a bend in degrees rounds well, and an IK rig consumes exactly this.\n8: *\n9: * Shared by tools/anim-import.mjs (Node: it measures a captured body, fits each clip, writes the set) and the\n10: * browser (src/mocap/mocap.js: it rebuilds every body point from a set to draw or drive a rig). No dependencies.\n11: *   const rest = MocapReadable.measure(captureClips)          // a body's proportions, from its own clips\n12: *   const { clip } = MocapReadable.fit(rest, capClip, 30)     // key poses within 30 mm of the capture\n13: *   MocapReadable.text(clip)  MocapReadable.parse(text)  MocapReadable.mirror(clip)\n14: *   MocapReadable.pose(rest, clip, t, out)                    // 36 body points (mm) at time t\n15: * ============================================================================= */\n26:// L and R are the figure's own sides. Directions are percentages and need not add up: [100, 0, 0] and [50, 0, 0] agree.\n27:// hips   [forward, right, up]   the pelvis, in percent of standing hip height (100 = standing, 55 = sitting on a chair)\n28:// body   [turn, lean, tilt]     the pelvis, degrees: turn + = toward the right, lean + = forward, tilt + = toward the right\n29:// chest  [turn, lean, tilt]     the chest, relative to the pelvis        head [turn, lean, tilt]  relative to the chest\n30:// shL shR    [reach, shrug]     degrees the shoulder swings forward (into a punch or an aim) and up, from rest\n31:// armL armR  [forward, out, up, bend, twist]  which way the arm points from the shoulder to the fist, in chest space\n32://            (out + = away from the body on that side; [0, 0, -100] hangs down, [100, 0, 0] punches ahead);\n33://            bend = elbow bend in degrees (0 = straight); twist = degrees the elbow swings round the arm from its\n34://            natural direction (back and down)\n35:// legL legR  [forward, out, up, bend, twist]  hip to ankle in pelvis space; bend: the knee; twist: from pointing forward\n36:// footL footR [down, out, toes]   degrees the foot points down from standing, turns outward, and the toes bend up\n37:// blade  [forward, right, up]   (sword clips) the direction of a blade held in the right fist, in chest space\n38:// root   [forward, right]       (clips that travel) how far the whole body has moved, in percent of standing hip height`;\n47:// a frame is [F, R, U]: its forward, right and up axes written in the parent's coordinates (f x r = u)\n55:// rotations as quaternions [x, y, z, w], to spread a bend over the spine and the neck\n106:// A twist is measured round the limb from the way its middle joint naturally bends. That direction is known for one\n107:// limb direction (REST: an arm hanging forward, out and down, its elbow bending back; a leg down, its knee forward)\n108:// and carried to the limb's actual direction by the shortest turn, so it moves smoothly with the limb everywhere\n109:// except straight opposite REST, where no arm or leg reaches (an arm behind the head across the back; a leg up behind).\n110:// (An earlier reference blended two fixed directions; where they faced apart it flipped, and in-betweens put the elbow\n111:// on the wrong side.) Both sides compute it from the stored direction, so they always agree.\n133:// the finger helpers only aim a blade: errors leave them out\n151: * measure(clips, tpose): proportions and rest offsets from the T-pose (or the first frame of the first clip), and how\n152: * much of the hips-to-chest turn each spine bone takes (and the neck between chest and head). That share differs per\n153: * library (how its animators posed the spine), so it is fitted from the clips themselves. The result is plain data.\n154: */\n288: * fit(R, cap, tol): a readable clip within tol mm of the capture. It starts from the position key frames and adds the\n289: * frame the readable in-betweening misses most, until the rest is within tol of what the format can capture at all\n290: * (a frame encoded as its own key): keys past the format's own limits are not worth their tokens.\n291: * Returns { clip, max, mean }: the worst and the average body-point error over the clip (mm).\n292: */","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01RZw4jUezLpndm656xeAA2P","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
