{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghenEpmVWtbdGD43gYwa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 114 checks pass in 66 seconds, including the browser check and the four AI Hub scenarios.\n\n## What changed\n\n- **Bump, set and spike** now play hand-keyed clips in the engine's readable format (`vendor/mocap/aoa-volley.js`). Each clip is a short sequence of key poses. The bump has the crouch, arms joined low and out, and the rise through the ball. The set has the hands above the forehead with the wrists back and the knees pushing. The spike has the approach, the jump, the arm cocked back and whipped over, and the landing.\n- **Arm IK** steps aside during those three moves, so the clip's arms show. It still places the hands for serves, blocks and high fives.\n- I checked in a browser that the title fighters play the three clips.\n\n## What is and isn't motion capture\n\nThe plan says these moves should be motion capture. I could not capture a volleyball bump, set or spike, because the library I cut from has no such moves. I hand-keyed them instead, in the same format and on the same body proportions as the captured clips, so they run through the same motion runtime. They are authored poses, not recordings. I can't honestly call them captured.\n\n## Verification\n\n`node test/run.mjs` passes 114 checks in 66 seconds, including the browser check of the file and the four AI Hub scenarios.\n\n## Sections\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-04 01:24:24\n- Source: Dead or Alive Xtreme Beach Volleyball (Team Ninja / Tecmo, 2003)\n- Plan: PLAN.md\n- Genre: sports (2v2 beach volleyball), with a light life sim and minigames\n- Perspective: broadcast side-on court view with a Serve Cam toggle; three-quarter view for walkable places; isometric island map\n- Tech: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (engine patches 2, 3 and 5), plain JavaScript, the engine's motion runtime, Playwright 1.63.0, Node 24\n- Art direction: PS1-style pixel art, sunset palette, a swimsuit outfit added to the engine\n- Animation: engine rig poses and attack moves; 20 captured clips from the engine's libraries; three hand-keyed clips (bump, set, spike); arm IK for serves, blocks and high fives\n- Audio: all synthesized in code, including Animalese voices and barks\n- Online: AI Hub shared-world session; each client moves its own fighter and sends claims that the host validates\n- Testing: the 114-check command described above\n- Research: engine docs and source, the AI Hub protocol and client; no web research\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); headless Chromium\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- Issues caught: about 16 game issues in the earlier rounds, plus this round's: the engine patch used a variable before it was declared (the engine's error screen caught it), and the location spawn read the wrong return shape.\n- Critical / game-breaking: two earlier (the Pool Hop slip that never recovered, and the dev-panel black screen); this round's location black screen also broke the montage, and is fixed.\n- Most helpful: the headless full-match run and the AI Hub harness. The zoomed browser capture was the only way to judge the clips and swimsuits.\n- Least helpful: screenshots at the game's native size, which can't show clip detail.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n- Liked most: the fixed-step clock and headless mode; the readable clip format, which can be written by hand; and the engine's outfit code, which accepted a new outfit cleanly.\n- Stuck or guessed wrong: the scene fade runs on game time, so a freeze set on entry stalls it. `TileMap.center` returns a pair. The motion runtime needs the engine loaded in the same context.\n- Most important to build myself: the referee, planner, timing judge, AI, text box with voices, vacation rules, attract director, swimsuit outfit patch, and the hand-keyed volleyball clips.\n- Hardest: making a ground-view match read as a side-on broadcast, and writing key poses that look right from the broadcast angle.\n- Engine changes: three, all marked `AOA:` (PlayStation symbols by default; a per-letter dialog voice hook the game does not use; the swimsuit outfit).\n- Change one thing: a volleyball clip library in the engine's motion set, so these moves can be captured rather than keyed by hand.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n- Liked most: the concrete numbers and the feature checklist, which made each gap easy to check.\n- Confusing: the plan and TASK.md disagreed on names and date; TASK.md was followed. The plan's scope is far larger than one session.\n- Hardest: the motion clips for bump, set and spike, which need a volleyball capture the libraries don't have.\n- Would change: name the motion source when the plan asks for clips (a library, a capture session, or hand-keyed poses), so the scope is fixed before the work starts.\n\nThe exit interview is `exit-interview.json` in the work folder. It parses and fills every field."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1352,"cache_read_input_tokens":795815,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1352},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"68c96722-ff85-4e7d-8e61-8c2d4dde53f4","uuid":"1b4e997c-a0a4-48c2-b329-3cf9c0059acb","timestamp":"2026-11-04T09:29:12.750Z","request_id":"req_011CghenEQxJ2USLpyxSnqiF"}
