ections\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-04 01:24:24\n- Source: Dead or Alive Xtreme Beach Volleyball (Team Ninja / Tecmo, 2003)\n- Plan: PLAN.md\n- Genre: sports (2v2 beach volleyball), with a light life sim and minigames\n- Perspective: broadcast side-on court view with a Serve Cam toggle; three-quarter view for walkable places; isometric island map\n- Tech: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (engine patches 2, 3 and 5), plain JavaScript, the engine's motion runtime, Playwright 1.63.0, Node 24\n- Art direction: PS1-style pixel art, sunset palette, a swimsuit outfit added to the engine, hair and hats drawn over the rig\n- Animation: engine rig poses and attack moves; 20 captured clips from the engine's libraries; three hand-keyed clips (bump, set, spike); arm IK for serves, blocks and high fives\n- Audio: all synthesized in code, including Animalese voices and barks\n- Online: AI Hub shared-world session; each client moves its own fighter and sends claims that the host validates\n- Testing: the 114-check command, including the browser check and the four AI Hub scenarios\n- Research: engine docs and source, the AI Hub protocol and client; no web research\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); headless Chromium\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- Issues caught: about 16 game issues in the earlier rounds, plus later ones: an engine patch used a variable before it was declared, the location spawn read the wrong return shape, and the beach map's padding used an undefined wall symbol.\n- Critical / game-breaking: the Pool Hop slip that never recovered and the dev-panel black screen (both earlier, both fixed); the location black screen, which also broke the montage (fixed).\n- Most helpful: the headless full-match run and the AI Hub harness.\n- Least helpful: screenshots at the game's native size, which can't show clip or swimsuit detail; zoomed captures were needed.\n\n**Engine Feedback**