{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgheuQCX3WA3pMbMZAuKH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've stopped working on this, and the goal is still not met as the hook defines it. I can't close the remaining gaps without a source or decision from you, so further rounds would only repeat the same report.\n\n## Current state\n\n- **Delivered:** `AheadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-DeadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-012424.html` in the work folder, and `exit-interview.json`, which parses and fills every field.\n- **Verified:** the full test command passes 114 checks in 65 seconds, including the browser check and the four AI Hub scenarios.\n- **Last change:** Pool Hop's wobble pads now settle one beat before the hop, as the plan specifies.\n\n## What is still simplified or partial\n\n- **Bump, set and spike:** hand-keyed poses, not captured motion-capture clips. The library I cut from has no volleyball moves.\n- **Hair and hats:** drawn as shapes over the rig, not new art.\n- **Attract montage:** real scenes driven by a bot, with short beats.\n- **Pool Hop:** the core rhythm is complete. The plan's twists are partly reflected: wobble pads flip a glyph, and double pads are judged.\n- **Final specials:** simplified versions of Golden Glare, House Edge and Lucky Seven.\n- **Online:** the host still runs the match, as the plan specifies. Clients move their own fighter and send touch claims, but there is no client-side prediction.\n\n## What would close them\n\nName a source for each item: a volleyball motion capture set, bespoke hair and hat art, and the full specials spec. Or tell me the current versions are acceptable as the implementation.\n\n## Sections\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-04 01:24:24\n- Source: Dead or Alive Xtreme Beach Volleyball (Team Ninja / Tecmo, 2003)\n- Plan: PLAN.md\n- Genre: sports (2v2 beach volleyball), with a light life sim and minigames\n- Perspective: broadcast side-on court view with a Serve Cam toggle; three-quarter view for walkable places; isometric island map\n- Tech: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (engine patches 2, 3 and 5), plain JavaScript, the engine's motion runtime, Playwright 1.63.0, Node 24\n- Art direction: PS1-style pixel art, sunset palette, a swimsuit outfit added to the engine, hair and hats drawn over the rig\n- Animation: engine rig poses and attack moves; 20 captured clips from the engine's libraries; three hand-keyed clips (bump, set, spike); arm IK for serves, blocks and high fives\n- Audio: all synthesized in code, including Animalese voices and barks\n- Online: AI Hub shared-world session; each client moves its own fighter and sends claims that the host validates\n- Testing: the 114-check command, including the browser check and the four AI Hub scenarios\n- Research: engine docs and source, the AI Hub protocol and client; no web research\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); headless Chromium\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- Issues caught: about 16 game issues in the earlier rounds, plus later ones: an engine patch used a variable before it was declared, the location spawn read the wrong return shape, and the beach map's padding used an undefined wall symbol.\n- Critical / game-breaking: the Pool Hop slip that never recovered and the dev-panel black screen (both earlier, both fixed); the location black screen, which also broke the montage (fixed).\n- Most helpful: the headless full-match run and the AI Hub harness.\n- Least helpful: screenshots at the game's native size, which can't show clip or swimsuit detail; zoomed captures were needed.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n- Liked most: the fixed-step clock and headless mode, the readable clip format (which can be written by hand), and the outfit code, which accepted a new swimsuit outfit cleanly.\n- Stuck or guessed wrong: the scene fade runs on game time, so a freeze set on entry stalls it. `TileMap.center` returns a pair. The motion runtime needs the engine in the same context.\n- Most important to build myself: the referee, planner, timing judge, AI, text box with voices, vacation rules, attract director, the swimsuit outfit patch, and the hand-keyed volleyball clips.\n- Hardest: making a ground-view match read as a side-on broadcast, and keying poses that read well from that angle.\n- Engine changes: three, all marked `AOA:` (PlayStation symbols by default; a per-letter dialog voice hook the game does not use; the swimsuit outfit).\n- Change one thing: a volleyball motion library in the engine's set, so these moves can be captured.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n- Liked most: the concrete numbers and the feature checklist, which made each gap easy to check.\n- Confusing: the plan and TASK.md disagreed on names and date; TASK.md was followed. The plan's scope is far larger than one session.\n- Hardest: the motion clips for bump, set and spike, which need a source the libraries don't have, and the full specials and art.\n- Would change: name the source for each plan item that needs one (a capture session, a library, bespoke art), and say up front whether approximations count, so the scope is fixed before the work starts."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":550,"cache_read_input_tokens":803771,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":550},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"68c96722-ff85-4e7d-8e61-8c2d4dde53f4","uuid":"c06934a7-a376-4a20-afbd-d8f6b3294d02","timestamp":"2026-11-04T09:30:48.425Z","request_id":"req_011CgheuPmUW4Ap34Mf2txbz"}
